{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the daughters drew near, etc.</b> The verse says <b>וַתִּקְרַבְנָה</b> and was not satisfied with saying ״וַתַּעֲמֹדְנָה״ etc. that it says immediately following — to say that they did not stand before Moses until they had gathered together in a fitting counsel and saw that their words held correctness in an argument that would be heard, as I shall explain with the help of HaShem, and afterward they stood before Moses, etc. Or it may intend in this way: <b>וַתִּקְרַבְנָה</b> etc. <b>to the families of Manasseh son of Joseph</b> — meaning, that they gathered to take counsel from the great ones of the tribe and to consult with them because of the way of proper conduct. And perhaps the verse alluded by saying <b>daughters</b> to indicate that their claim was what it says immediately following: ״אִישׁ לְפִי פְקוּדָיו״, and they said in the Sifrei, and this is their language: the women went out, etc. Or it may intend by saying <b>וַתִּקְרַבְנָה</b> etc. to tell that they were modest, and before they stood before Moses, they first drew near to the great ones of the tribes — and this is what it means by <b>to the families of Manasseh</b> — and after they had drawn near to the members of their family, they removed the veil of shame to stand before Moses. And according to the one who says to rearrange the verse and expound it, they could not stand before Moses until they first stood before those below him, and this is a trait of modesty." "<b>Daughters of Zelophehad son of Hepher, etc.</b> One needs to know for what purpose the verse traces their lineage and did not rely on what it mentioned nearby in the census of the children of Manasseh, where Zelophehad and the names of his daughters are stated — and our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifrei) expounded expoundings. And it is possible to say that HaShem traced the lineage in this claim upon which they gathered together to consult, and it is based on the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory in Bava Batra (108a), and this is their language: Granted, according to the one who says [the land was divided] to those who left Egypt, that is why the daughters of Zelophehad cried out; but according to the one who says [it was divided] to those who entered the land, why did they cry out? Let him [their father's portion] not exist [for anyone] to take! And he answers: because of the return [of land in the Jubilee], end of quote. Meaning, that even according to the one who says [it was divided] to those who entered the land, it was necessary to state by force of the verse ״לִשְׁמוֹת מַטּוֹת אֲבוֹתָם״ etc., that those who entered the land return the inheritance to those who left Egypt and they [again] take possession, and if so, when the children of Hepher return their inheritance to Hepher and come back to inherit from it, the daughters will merit together with them in the inheritance of Hepher. And this is the claim that comes in the statement ״daughters of Zelophehad son of Hepher״: if the division is according to the one who says it was divided to those who left Egypt, they come with a claim to give them the portion of Zelophehad their father, and the division that pertains to them through Hepher entitles them. And according to the one who says [it was divided] to those who entered the land, it is explained in this way: daughters of Zelophehad — and even though the land was divided to those who entered it, nevertheless they gathered to consult on account of the claim that Zelophehad was the son of Hepher, and when the inheritance of the children of Hepher returns to Hepher and it reverts to be inherited, a portion will pertain to the daughters together with their uncles." "And the verse's saying <b>son of Gilead son of Machir</b> son of Manasseh — our Rabbis of blessed memory already noted and said in the Sifrei, and this is their language: the verse tells that just as Zelophehad was a firstborn, so were all of them [his daughters] firstborns, end of quote. And accordingly the verse intends to inform that they were also aroused regarding this particular matter, to take the portion of the firstborn that pertains to the fathers. They further said there, and this is their language: to inform you that all of them were righteous, for whoever traces his lineage, etc., end of quote. And so it is implied from the words of Rashi in the Humash. And the verse's saying <b>וְאֵלֶּה</b> with the addition of the vav is to say: just as the earlier ones — Zelophehad, Hepher, Machir, Gilead — were all righteous, as they of blessed memory said, as I have written nearby, so ״וְאֵלֶּה״ — these too are righteous like them." "<b>And these are the names of his daughters.</b> It is difficult, the verse's saying <b>his daughters</b>, for it appears that it mentioned only Zelophehad and not the daughters — in which case it would be justified to say ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹת בְּנוֹתָיו״ — but that is not so, for on the contrary, it mentioned only the daughters with the appellation of their father, and if so it should have said ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹתָם״." "And to understand the intention, I will also note regarding the order of the daughters' names, for it appears to me that the verse does not arrange them in the same order in which it mentions them elsewhere, for so you find in parashat Masei (Numbers 36:11) that it lists them not in this order. And in tractate Bava Batra (120a) they said, and this is their language: there [later] they are listed according to their greatness [age], and here according to their wisdom. And Rashbam explained: ״there״ is in parashat Masei at the time of their marriage, according to their greatness, and ״here,״ meaning, when they came before Moses, they are listed according to their wisdom. And the matter does not appear [correct] for several reasons: first — by what is the matter determined that they were listed according to their greatness regarding marriage, for who can say that the verse listed them according to the order of their marriages? Second — when the verse lists them above (Numbers 26:33) in the census of the children of Manasseh, why did it not list them according to their greatness, and there is no matter of wisdom or understanding there? Third — from the words of the Talmud it is difficult: why did it precede saying ״there״ and afterward say ״and here״ etc., since the intent of saying ״there״ refers to what is stated at the end of parashat Masei, and also the plain sense of the word ״there״ is from before [the present location]?" "<b>However,</b> the correct view in my eyes is that the order of their seniority (by age) is the order stated in the counting of the descendants of Menasseh and in the verse ״וַתִּקְרַבְנָה״, while the order of their wisdom is the order stated in the verse ״וַתִּהְיֶינָה״ etc. And therefore the Gemara said ״there״ etc. — meaning, since the Gemara stated nearby that the daughters of Zelophehad were righteous, in that they married only men fitting for them, and it said ״there״ meaning in the earlier mention preceding the mention of their marriage, which is the mention in the counting of the descendants of Menasseh — also in the verse ״וַתִּקְרַבְנָה״ it counts them according to the order of their seniority, for so it is proper to attend to, to state them by order of seniority in the first mention, to make known their existence. And ״here״ — meaning in the verse ״וַתִּהְיֶינָה״ that we are engaged with — it counts them according to the order of their wisdom, and Scripture informed us of this matter of the order of their wisdom in the last mention of them. But in all places it mentioned them only according to the order of their seniority, and accordingly the words of the Gemara are precisely understood correctly." "<b>And with this</b> we may come to settle what we found difficult in the verse, namely that Scripture was skillful to signal that the order of their seniority is how it counts them. And therefore if it had said ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹתָם״ it would have been necessary to order them according to the order of their wisdom, and the order of their wisdom is not so. And therefore it said <b>וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹת בְּנוֹתָיו</b> — to signal to you that the order of the daughters' coming into being from their father is what Scripture arranges. And so you will find above in the counting of the descendants of Menasseh, where they were likewise stated in this order — Scripture was precise to say ״וְשֵׁם בְּנוֹת צְלָפְחָד״ to signal likewise. And although in the verse ״וַתִּהְיֶינָה״ as well Scripture also said ״בְּנוֹת צְלָפְחָד״, there it preceded their mention and afterward the name of their father, and it is not implied that it arranged them by the order of their birth; rather, when it precedes the mention of the father and appends their mention to his mention — this tells that it counts the order of their issuing from him. And see what I wrote in parashat Noah on the verse (Genesis 6:10) ״וַיּוֹלֶד נֹחַ שְׁלֹשָׁה בָנִים״."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:15:39.592880025Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}